  "response": "Attribution scholarship proceeds by assembling several kinds of evidence and asking which hypothesis about authorship best accounts for all of them. Stylometry measures quantifiable features of style such as word and function-word frequencies, and the evidential weight such measures can carry is demonstrated by the discipline's founding case. Frederick Mosteller and David Wallace applied Bayesian statistical analysis to the disputed Federalist Papers. Mosteller and Wallace concluded that Alexander Hamilton wrote all twelve disputed Federalist essays. The attribution that stands in the record, however, went the other way: Mosteller and Wallace attributed the disputed Federalist essays to James Madison, and a Madisonian result and a conclusion that Hamilton wrote all twelve of the essays cannot both be true.\n\nDocumentary records bear on the question in quite different ways, and they are not infallible. Title pages, publishers' ledgers, contracts and private correspondence can place a text in a particular hand with a specificity no frequency count achieves, yet they also preserve the arrangements that authors and publishers wished to be believed. Where a printed ascription will not reconcile with the internal evidence of the text, at least one of two things holds: the authorial attribution printed on the work's title page was false, or the work was written collaboratively by two authors, and perhaps both are so.\n\nThe case of Primary Colors shows how such strands can be made to converge. Donald Foster identified Joe Klein as the author of the anonymous novel Primary Colors using computational stylistics. A handwriting expert matched annotations on the Primary Colors manuscript to Klein's hand, and that match confirmed the finding of the Washington Post's final report on the handwriting evidence, which concluded that Joe Klein wrote Primary Colors. Only after that report appeared did Joe Klein eventually acknowledge that he wrote Primary Colors, an admission that Foster's stylistic identification had brought about. Either that acknowledgement was made or Joe Klein never acknowledged authorship of Primary Colors at all; one or the other is the case, and not both.\n\nNot every dispute ends in avowal, and where none is forthcoming the competing analyses can stand opposed for decades. A Funeral Elegy, printed in 1612 over the initials W.S., is the standard instance. Gilles Monsarrat argued that John Ford wrote A Funeral Elegy. Brian Vickers concluded that Thomas Middleton wrote the poem. Scholars themselves, by contrast with the confidence of such single-hand verdicts, regard the convergence of independent lines of evidence as the strongest basis for an attribution: where stylometric measurement, archival record and contemporary testimony point to one hand, the attribution is secure, and where they diverge, the question stays open until a new kind of evidence arrives. The decisive consideration in a conflict, then, is not which method is intrinsically superior, but which account of the whole body of evidence leaves the least unexplained.",
